-- Migration 18 -> 19: cache the WHOLE recurrence rule on the three recurring kinds (#382).
--
-- `recurrence_type` + `recurrence_days` could express only "a frequency and, if weekly, some weekdays".
-- Every other cadence parameter and the entire `end` bound were dropped, so a cache round-trip did not
-- merely fail to render a rule — it DESTROYED it. An every-30-days chore and an every-29-days chore were
-- the same row; `every_n_days` and `custom` had no domain representation at all and persisted as the
-- literal enum name "Unknown", from which the original cadence was unrecoverable.
--
-- Twelve flat nullable primitives per table, NOT one `recurrence_json` blob. The three tables state a
-- no-adapters invariant (every column a SQL primitive, all row<->domain conversion in Kotlin); a blob
-- would breach it, and would also make "find every item recurring monthly" impossible without a
-- full-table Kotlin scan.
--
-- Which columns are meaningful is decided by `recurrence_type` — all are NULL for a cadence that does
-- not use them, which is the same value a pre-migration row carries, so **no back-fill is needed**: a
-- row written before this migration decodes to exactly the rule it already had (frequency + days, no
-- parameters, the Never bound) and is repopulated on the next `/items` refresh. Contrast 16->17's
-- `occurred_at`, which had to be back-filled because NULL sorted it out of reach.
--
-- Three column-group notes, each load-bearing:
--   * `recurrence_interval` is the cycle multiplier for BOTH wire spellings — `every_n_days.n` and
--     `monthly`/`yearly`'s `interval`. They are one domain concept (the multiplier; the frequency
--     supplies the unit) and can never co-occur on the wire.
--   * the `recurrence_anchor_*` trio (the monthly `on` anchor) is kept SEPARATE from the yearly
--     `recurrence_month`/`recurrence_day` pair. Monthly and yearly are mutually exclusive, so one shared
--     "day" column would fit — but then a monthly row would decode with BOTH an anchor and a stray
--     day-of-month, and the write->read round-trip would no longer be an identity.
--   * a NULL `recurrence_end_type` reads as the Never bound. That mirrors the wire exactly, where an
--     absent `end` key IS never (the backend's `Serialize` skips it) — so NULL is the correct reading
--     here, not merely a safe fallback.
--
-- `recurrence_raw_type` holds the wire token of a cadence this client version cannot model, so an
-- additive future cadence survives the cache and the Backup file under its own name instead of being
-- flattened to "Unknown" and made unrestorable.
--
-- The `*_type` columns store the DOMAIN sealed-subtype name (`DayOfMonth`/`NthWeekday`,
-- `Never`/`OnDate`/`AfterCount`), like `definition_state` and `priority` — the wire tokens stay in
-- core:network (ADR-0011). The decoders degrade an unrecognised token rather than throwing.
--
-- Plain forward ADD COLUMN, in the same order as the CREATE TABLE tail (ADD COLUMN appends, so the
-- order is part of the schema). Never edit a released migration or snapshot — ADR-0022 runbook.
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_interval INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_type TEXT;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_day INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_nth INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_weekday TEXT;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_month INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_day INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_rrule TEXT;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_end_type TEXT;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_end_date TEXT;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_end_count INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE habitEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_raw_type TEXT;

ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_interval INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_type TEXT;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_day INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_nth INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_weekday TEXT;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_month INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_day INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_rrule TEXT;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_end_type TEXT;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_end_date TEXT;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_end_count INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE choreEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_raw_type TEXT;

ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_interval INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_type TEXT;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_day INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_nth INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_anchor_weekday TEXT;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_month INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_day INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_rrule TEXT;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_end_type TEXT;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_end_date TEXT;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_end_count INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE eventEntity ADD COLUMN recurrence_raw_type TEXT;
